---
code: verdict-terminal-pipeline
when-fires: a `__is_converged` body's last status-bearing statement is a PIPELINE, so the answering status is the tail stage's. lint/source_verdict.rs. No params - the span says which body.
why: rul-rc-partition - a verdict function's exit status reads one fixed table, so a tail stage's status flattens `1 = complement` and `>=2 = cannot-say` into whatever the tail produced. FALSIFICATION-FIRST (rul-unprovable-rides-the-vouch): the check may under-report, never gate.
edit-loop: edit a sentence in the transcript below, then run `mise run loom:compile -- verdict-terminal-pipeline && mise run loom:promote -- verdict-terminal-pipeline`; prove it stuck with `mise run test`, which is also what catches anything else the promotion moved. `mise run loom:vars -- --all verdict-terminal-pipeline` lists this case's values; type {{name}} to insert or move one.
---
-- oracle.sh --
# dorc-lang/v0.2
foo__is_converged() {
foo --status "$1" | grep -q ok
}

-- replay --
$ dorc lint oracle.sh --no-tools
dorc lint findings (advisory; the machine format `--format=jsonl` is the stable surface):

oracle.sh:
  3:1 warn [verdict-body:verdict-terminal-pipeline] sm this __is_converged body answers with a pipeline's tail status (oracle-contract section 3): the exit status is the LAST stage's, not necessarily the tool-under-description's -- prefer a shape where the modeled tool produces the status directly.

dorc lint: 0 errors, 1 warning, 0 infos across 1 file.
